## The Clarity Escalator

In a round brilliant diamond, the difference between VS2 and VVS1 is nearly invisible. Both look clean to the naked eye. The 58 angled facets scatter light and obscure anything hiding inside the stone.

In an Asscher cut, the same clarity comparison produces a completely different result. The step facets act like a window — light travels straight through, and anything in the path is visible. This creates a clear and predictable escalation effect: each clarity grade up removes a visually meaningful layer of inclusion from the view.

This is why the clarity floor for Asscher rings is higher than for any brilliant-cut diamond. VS1 and VS2 grades that would look clean in a round will often show inclusions in normal candlelight in an Asscher. The escalator only starts working once you reach VVS2.

| Clarity | What you see in Asscher | What this means |
|---|---|---|
| SI1–SI2 | Inclusions visible to naked eye, no magnification needed | Never buy for an Asscher engagement ring |
| VS2 | Most stones show visible inclusions in direct light | Below the clarity floor — avoid unless examined in person |
| VS1 | Borderline — depends entirely on inclusion type and position | Risky without inspection; some pass, most don't |
| VVS2 | Clean to naked eye in all lighting conditions | **The clarity floor — minimum acceptable for Asscher** |
| VVS1 | Completely clean, high confidence | Step up from floor — worth it for platinum builds |
| IF / FL | Perfectly clean by all standards | Collector grade — meaningful only if natural provenance matters |

**Verdict:** Never buy a natural Asscher below VVS2 without inspecting the actual stone in person. The certificate tells you the grade — it does not tell you where the inclusion sits. An inclusion in the corner of a VS1 Asscher may be hidden by a prong. An inclusion under the table facet will broadcast in every lighting condition.

Read the grading report. Look at the actual stone image if buying online. The certificate is a starting point, not a final answer.

> **Farzana's take:** I had a client bring me a VS1 Asscher she bought for her engagement ring from a discount retailer. It was a G-VS1 — exactly what the internet told her to buy. There was a cloud inclusion dead center under the table. In her kitchen light, it looked like a frost patch. She thought the stone was fake. It was not fake — it was just the wrong clarity grade for this cut. VVS2 is the line. Stay above it.

