## Expert Summary

The asscher cut diamond engagement ring is one of the most timeless purchases in fine jewelry. The cut has not changed meaningfully since 1902. The aesthetic it rewards — geometric, vintage, platinum, milgrain — has a 124-year track record.

It is not a trend. It will not become one.

The buying rules are just as clear. VVS2 is the clarity floor, not VS2. Art Deco settings are the correct home for this stone.

Four-prong solitaires clip the octagonal character. Lab-grown Asschers at 1.5–2ct give you more visual diamond per dollar than almost anything else on the market right now.

If you are buying a natural Asscher ring, budget for the clarity upgrade. A [GIA 1.07ct F-VVS1](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28382007?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at [$2,826](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28382007?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is almost always a better purchase than a D-VS2 at [$3,580](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/17719310?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

Better clarity, larger stone, lower cost. The D color adds nothing at this budget. The F-VVS1 looks cleaner in every lighting condition.

If you are buying a lab Asscher ring, the [2ct D-FL at $5,510](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29446220?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with a [vintage platinum setting at $2,090](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/enchanted-scroll-vintage-style-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-315306?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) for a $7,600 total ring is one of the strongest value propositions in engagement jewelry right now. Natural equivalent at comparable spec: $24,000+.

For a full deep-dive into cut proportions, Hall of Mirrors explained, and Asscher vs emerald cut buying criteria, read the [complete Asscher cut diamond guide](/asscher-cut-diamond).

